data: {"index":1,"delta":{"name":"microsoft_learn:microsoft_docs_search","is_error":false,"result":{"structured_content":{"results":[{"title":"What is Azure Functions?","contentUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview","content":"# What is Azure Functions?\nAzure Functions is a serverless solution that allows you to build robust apps while using less code, and with less infrastructure and lower costs. Instead of worrying about deploying and maintaining servers, you can use the cloud infrastructure to provide all the up-to-date resources needed to keep your applications running.\nYou focus on the code that matters most to you, in the most productive language for you, and Azure Functions handles the rest. For a list of supported languages, see [Supported languages in Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/supported-languages)."},{"title":"Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-csharp)","contentUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql#samples","content":"# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-csharp)\n## Samples\nIn addition to the samples for C#, Java, JavaScript, PowerShell, and Python available in the [Azure SQL bindings GitHub repository](https://github.com/Azure/azure-functions-sql-extension/tree/main/samples), more are available in Azure Samples:\n1. [ToDo API sample with Azure SQL bindings (C#)](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/azure-samples/azure-sql-binding-func-dotnet-todo/todo-backend-dotnet-azure-sql-bindings-azure-functions/)\n2. [Use SQL bindings in Azure Stream Analytics](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/stream-analytics/sql-database-upsert#option-1-update-by-key-with-the-azure-function-sql-binding)\n# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-javascript)\nThis set of articles explains how to work with [Azure SQL](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/index) bindings in Azure Functions. Azure Functions supports input bindings, output bindings, and a function trigger for the Azure SQL and SQL Server products.\n| Action | Type| \n|  --- | ---  |\n| Trigger a function when a change is detected on a SQL table | [SQL trigger](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-trigger) |\n| Read data from a database | [Input binding](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-input) |\n| Save data to a database | [Output binding](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-output) |\n"},{"content":"# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-javascript)\n## Samples\nIn addition to the samples for C#, Java, JavaScript, PowerShell, and Python available in the [Azure SQL bindings GitHub repository](https://github.com/Azure/azure-functions-sql-extension/tree/main/samples), more are available in Azure Samples:\n1. [ToDo API sample with Azure SQL bindings (C#)](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/azure-samples/azure-sql-binding-func-dotnet-todo/todo-backend-dotnet-azure-sql-bindings-azure-functions/)\n2. [Use SQL bindings in Azure Stream Analytics](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/stream-analytics/sql-database-upsert#option-1-update-by-key-with-the-azure-function-sql-binding)\n# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-powershell)\nThis set of articles explains how to work with [Azure SQL](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/index) bindings in Azure Functions. Azure Functions supports input bindings, output bindings, and a function trigger for the Azure SQL and SQL Server products.\n| Action | Type| \n|  --- | ---  |\n| Trigger a function when a change is detected on a SQL table | [SQL trigger](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-trigger) |\n| Read data from a database | [Input binding](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-input) |\n| Save data to a database | [Output binding](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-output) |\n","contentUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql#samples","title":"Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-javascript)"},{"content":"# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-powershell)\n## Samples\nIn addition to the samples for C#, Java, JavaScript, PowerShell, and Python available in the [Azure SQL bindings GitHub repository](https://github.com/Azure/azure-functions-sql-extension/tree/main/samples), more are available in Azure Samples:\n1. [ToDo API sample with Azure SQL bindings (C#)](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/azure-samples/azure-sql-binding-func-dotnet-todo/todo-backend-dotnet-azure-sql-bindings-azure-functions/)\n2. [Use SQL bindings in Azure Stream Analytics](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/stream-analytics/sql-database-upsert#option-1-update-by-key-with-the-azure-function-sql-binding)\n# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-python)\nThis set of articles explains how to work with [Azure SQL](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/index) bindings in Azure Functions. Azure Functions supports input bindings, output bindings, and a function trigger for the Azure SQL and SQL Server products.\n| Action | Type| \n|  --- | ---  |\n| Trigger a function when a change is detected on a SQL table | [SQL trigger](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-trigger) |\n| Read data from a database | [Input binding](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-input) |\n| Save data to a database | [Output binding](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-output) |\n","title":"Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-powershell)","contentUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql#samples"},{"title":"Azure Functions libraries for .NET","content":"# Azure Functions libraries for .NET\n## Overview\n[Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview) is a solution for easily running small pieces of code, or *functions*, in Azure. Azure Functions supports a [serverless](https://azure.microsoft.com/solutions/serverless/) execution model.\n## Durable Functions extension\n*Durable Functions* is an extension of [Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview) that lets you write stateful functions in a serverless compute environment. The extension lets you define stateful workflows by writing [*orchestrator functions*](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/durable/durable-functions-orchestrations) and stateful entities by writing [*entity functions*](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/durable/durable-functions-entities) using the Azure Functions programming model. Behind the scenes, the extension manages state, checkpoints, and restarts for you, allowing you to focus on your business logic.\nInstall the extension [NuGet package](https://www.nuget.org/packages/Microsoft.Azure.WebJobs.Extensions.DurableTask) directly from the Visual Studio [Package Manager console](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/nuget/tools/package-manager-console) or with the [.NET Core CLI](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/tools/dotnet-add-package).\nThe Azure API reference documentation supports [version 2.x of the Durable Functions extension](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/overview/azure/functions/runtime).\n### Visual Studio package manager\n```powershell\nInstall-Package Microsoft.Azure.WebJobs.Extensions.DurableTask\n```\n### .NET Core CLI\n```dotnetcli\ndotnet add package Microsoft.Azure.WebJobs.Extensions.DurableTask\n```\n### Getting started\n## Samples\nView the [complete list](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/browse/?products=azure-functions&languages=csharp) of Azure Functions samples.","contentUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/overview/azure/functions?view=azure-dotnet#overview"},{"content":"# Overview of Azure functions for Azure Redis (programming-language-java)\nThis article describes how to use either Azure Managed Redis or Azure Cache for Redis with Azure Functions to create optimized serverless and event-driven architectures.\nAzure Functions provides an event-driven programming model where triggers and bindings are key features. With Azure Functions, you can easily build event-driven serverless applications. Azure Redis services (Azure Managed Redis and Azure Cache for Redis) provide a set of building blocks and best practices for building distributed applications, including microservices, state management, pub/sub messaging, and more.\nAzure Redis can be used as a trigger for Azure Functions, allowing you to initiate a serverless workflow. This functionality can be highly useful in data architectures like a write-behind cache, or any event-based architectures.\nYou can integrate Azure Redis and Azure Functions to build functions that react to events from Azure Redis or external systems.\n| Action | Direction| \n|  --- | ---  |\n| [Trigger on Redis pub sub messages](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-cache-trigger-redispubsub) | Trigger |\n| [Trigger on Redis lists](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-cache-trigger-redislist) | Trigger |\n| [Trigger on Redis streams](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-cache-trigger-redisstream) | Trigger |\n| [Read a cached value](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-cache-input) | Input |\n| [Write a values to cache](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-cache-output) | Output |\n\n## Scope of availability for functions triggers and bindings\n| Tier | Azure Cache for Redis (Basic, Standard, Premium, Enterprise, Enterprise Flash) | Azure Managed Redis (Memory Optimized, Basic, Compute Optimized, Flash Optimized)| \n|  --- | --- | ---  |\n| Pub/Sub | Yes | Yes |\n| Lists | Yes | Yes |\n| Streams | Yes | Yes |\n| Bindings | Yes | Yes |\n\nImportant\nRedis triggers are currently only supported for functions running in either a [Elastic Premium plan](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-premium-plan) or a dedicated [App Service plan](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/dedicated-plan).","contentUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-cache","title":"Overview of Azure functions for Azure Redis (programming-language-java)"},{"title":"Get started with Azure Functions (programming-language-csharp)","content":"# Get started with Azure Functions (programming-language-csharp)\nAzure Functions lets you implement your system's logic as event-driven, readily available blocks of code. These code blocks are called functions. This article helps you find the most useful Azure Functions content quickly. For more general information about Azure Functions, see the [Introduction to Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview).\nChoose your preferred development language at the top of the article.\n## Create your first function\nComplete a quickstart article to create and deploy your first functions in less than five minutes.\nYou can create your first function by using one of the following tools:\n1. [Azure Developer CLI (azd)](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/create-first-function-azure-developer-cli)\n2. [Command line](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-azure-cli)\n3. [Visual Studio Code](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-vs-code)\n4. [Visual Studio](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-create-your-first-function-visual-studio)\n## Review end-to-end samples\nThese sites let you browse existing functions reference projects and samples in your desired language:\n1. [Awesome azd template library](https://azure.github.io/awesome-azd/?tags=functions&tags=dotnetCsharp)\n2. [Azure Functions samples in GitHub](https://github.com/search?q=topic%3Aazure-functions+org%3AAzure-Samples+fork%3Atrue+language%3AC%23&type=repositories&l=C%23)\n3. [Azure samples browser](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/browse/?expanded=azure&languages=csharp&products=azure-functions)\n## Scenarios\nHere are some common scenarios to help you get started:\n1. [Process file uploads](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#process-file-uploads)\n2. [Real-time stream and event processing](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#real-time-stream-and-event-processing)\n3. [Build AI-enabled apps](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-ai-enabled-apps)\n4. [Run scheduled tasks](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#run-scheduled-tasks)\n5. [Build a scalable web API](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-a-scalable-web-api)\n6. [Build a serverless workflow](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-a-serverless-workflow)\n7. [Respond to database changes](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#respond-to-database-changes)\n8. [Create reliable message systems](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#create-reliable-message-systems)","contentUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-get-started"},{"contentUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-get-started","content":"# Get started with Azure Functions (programming-language-javascript)\nAzure Functions lets you implement your system's logic as event-driven, readily available blocks of code. These code blocks are called functions. This article helps you find the most useful Azure Functions content quickly. For more general information about Azure Functions, see the [Introduction to Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview).\nChoose your preferred development language at the top of the article.\n## Create your first function\nComplete a quickstart article to create and deploy your first functions in less than five minutes.\nYou can create your first function by using one of the following tools:\n1. [Azure Developer CLI (azd)](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/create-first-function-azure-developer-cli)\n2. [Command line](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-azure-cli)\n3. [Visual Studio Code](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-vs-code)\n## Review end-to-end samples\nThese sites let you browse existing functions reference projects and samples in your desired language:\n1. [Awesome azd template library](https://azure.github.io/awesome-azd/?tags=functions&tags=javascript)\n2. [Azure Functions samples in GitHub](https://github.com/search?q=topic%3Aazure-functions+org%3AAzure-Samples+fork%3Atrue+language%3AJavaScript&type=repositories)\n3. [Azure samples browser](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/browse/?expanded=azure&languages=javascript&products=azure-functions)\n## Scenarios\nHere are some common scenarios to help you get started:\n1. [Process file uploads](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#process-file-uploads)\n2. [Real-time stream and event processing](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#real-time-stream-and-event-processing)\n3. [Build AI-enabled apps](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-ai-enabled-apps)\n4. [Run scheduled tasks](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#run-scheduled-tasks)\n5. [Build a scalable web API](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-a-scalable-web-api)\n6. [Build a serverless workflow](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-a-serverless-workflow)\n7. [Respond to database changes](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#respond-to-database-changes)\n8. [Create reliable message systems](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#create-reliable-message-systems)","title":"Get started with Azure Functions (programming-language-javascript)"},{"content":"# Azure Functions diagnostics overview\nWhen you’re running a function app, you want to be prepared for any issues that may arise, from 4xx errors to trigger failures. Azure Functions diagnostics is an intelligent and interactive experience to help you troubleshoot your function app with no configuration or extra cost. When you do run into issues with your function app, Azure Functions diagnostics points out what’s wrong. It guides you to the right information to more easily and quickly troubleshoot and resolve the issue. This article shows you the basics of how to use Azure Functions diagnostics to more quickly diagnose and solve common function app issues.\n## Start Azure Functions diagnostics\nTo start Azure Functions diagnostics:\n1. Navigate to your function app in the [Azure portal](https://portal.azure.com).\n2. Select **Diagnose and solve problems** to open Azure Functions diagnostics.\n3. Choose a category that best describes the issue of your function app by using the keywords in the homepage tile. You can also type a keyword that best describes your issue in the search bar. For example, you could type `execution` to see a list of diagnostic reports related to your function app execution and open them directly from the homepage.\n ![Search for Azure Functions diagnostics.](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/media/functions-diagnostics/functions-app-search-azure-functions-diagnostics.png)\n## Use the Interactive interface\nOnce you select a homepage category that best aligns with your function app's problem, Azure Functions diagnostics' interactive interface, named Genie, can guide you through diagnosing and solving problem of your app. You can use the tile shortcuts provided by Genie to view the full diagnostic report of the problem category that you're interested in. The tile shortcuts provide you a direct way of accessing your diagnostic metrics.\n![Genie is Azure Functions diagnostics' interface.](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/media/functions-diagnostics/genie.png)\nAfter selecting a tile, you can see a list of topics related to the issue described in the tile. These topics provide snippets of notable information from the full report. Select any of these topics to investigate the issues further. Also, you can select **View Full Report** to explore all the topics on a single page.\n![Preview of diagnostic report](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/media/functions-diagnostics/preview-of-diagnostic-report.png)","contentUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-diagnostics","title":"Azure Functions diagnostics overview"},{"title":"Get started with Azure Functions (programming-language-go)","contentUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-get-started","content":"# Get started with Azure Functions (programming-language-go)\nAzure Functions lets you implement your system's logic as event-driven, readily available blocks of code. These code blocks are called functions. This article helps you find the most useful Azure Functions content quickly. For more general information about Azure Functions, see the [Introduction to Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview).\nChoose your preferred development language at the top of the article.\n## Create your first function\nComplete a quickstart article to create and deploy your first functions in less than five minutes.\nYou can create your first function by using one of the following tools:\n1. [Azure Developer CLI (azd)](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/create-first-function-azure-developer-cli)\n2. [Command line](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-azure-cli)\n3. [Visual Studio Code](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-vs-code)\nGet started with Go on Azure Functions:\n1. [Command line quickstart](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-azure-cli?pivots=programming-language-go)\n## Review end-to-end samples\n1. [Azure Functions Go worker samples](https://github.com/Azure/azure-functions-golang-worker/tree/main/samples)\n## Scenarios\nHere are some common scenarios to help you get started:\n1. [Process file uploads](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#process-file-uploads)\n2. [Real-time stream and event processing](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#real-time-stream-and-event-processing)\n3. [Build AI-enabled apps](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-ai-enabled-apps)\n4. [Run scheduled tasks](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#run-scheduled-tasks)\n5. [Build a scalable web API](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-a-scalable-web-api)\n6. [Build a serverless workflow](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-a-serverless-workflow)\n7. [Respond to database changes](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#respond-to-database-changes)\n8. [Create reliable message systems](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#create-reliable-message-systems)\n## Explore an interactive tutorial\nComplete one of the following interactive training modules to learn more about Functions:\n1. [Well-Architected Framework - Performance efficiency](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/azure-well-architected-performance-efficiency/)\n2. [Azure Functions triggers and bindings](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-triggers-bindings)\nTo learn even more, see the [full listing of interactive tutorials](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/browse/?expanded=azure&products=azure-functions)."}]},"result":"{\"results\":[{\"title\":\"What is Azure Functions?\",\"content\":\"# What is Azure Functions?\\nAzure Functions is a serverless solution that allows you to build robust apps while using less code, and with less infrastructure and lower costs. Instead of worrying about deploying and maintaining servers, you can use the cloud infrastructure to provide all the up-to-date resources needed to keep your applications running.\\nYou focus on the code that matters most to you, in the most productive language for you, and Azure Functions handles the rest. For a list of supported languages, see [Supported languages in Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/supported-languages).\",\"contentUrl\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview\"},{\"title\":\"Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-csharp)\",\"content\":\"# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-csharp)\\n## Samples\\nIn addition to the samples for C#, Java, JavaScript, PowerShell, and Python available in the [Azure SQL bindings GitHub repository](https://github.com/Azure/azure-functions-sql-extension/tree/main/samples), more are available in Azure Samples:\\n1. [ToDo API sample with Azure SQL bindings (C#)](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/azure-samples/azure-sql-binding-func-dotnet-todo/todo-backend-dotnet-azure-sql-bindings-azure-functions/)\\n2. [Use SQL bindings in Azure Stream Analytics](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/stream-analytics/sql-database-upsert#option-1-update-by-key-with-the-azure-function-sql-binding)\\n# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-javascript)\\nThis set of articles explains how to work with [Azure SQL](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/index) bindings in Azure Functions. Azure Functions supports input bindings, output bindings, and a function trigger for the Azure SQL and SQL Server products.\\n| Action | Type| \\n|  --- | ---  |\\n| Trigger a function when a change is detected on a SQL table | [SQL trigger](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-trigger) |\\n| Read data from a database | [Input binding](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-input) |\\n| Save data to a database | [Output binding](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-output) |\\n\",\"contentUrl\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql#samples\"},{\"title\":\"Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-javascript)\",\"content\":\"# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-javascript)\\n## Samples\\nIn addition to the samples for C#, Java, JavaScript, PowerShell, and Python available in the [Azure SQL bindings GitHub repository](https://github.com/Azure/azure-functions-sql-extension/tree/main/samples), more are available in Azure Samples:\\n1. [ToDo API sample with Azure SQL bindings (C#)](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/azure-samples/azure-sql-binding-func-dotnet-todo/todo-backend-dotnet-azure-sql-bindings-azure-functions/)\\n2. [Use SQL bindings in Azure Stream Analytics](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/stream-analytics/sql-database-upsert#option-1-update-by-key-with-the-azure-function-sql-binding)\\n# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-powershell)\\nThis set of articles explains how to work with [Azure SQL](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/index) bindings in Azure Functions. Azure Functions supports input bindings, output bindings, and a function trigger for the Azure SQL and SQL Server products.\\n| Action | Type| \\n|  --- | ---  |\\n| Trigger a function when a change is detected on a SQL table | [SQL trigger](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-trigger) |\\n| Read data from a database | [Input binding](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-input) |\\n| Save data to a database | [Output binding](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-output) |\\n\",\"contentUrl\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql#samples\"},{\"title\":\"Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-powershell)\",\"content\":\"# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-powershell)\\n## Samples\\nIn addition to the samples for C#, Java, JavaScript, PowerShell, and Python available in the [Azure SQL bindings GitHub repository](https://github.com/Azure/azure-functions-sql-extension/tree/main/samples), more are available in Azure Samples:\\n1. [ToDo API sample with Azure SQL bindings (C#)](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/azure-samples/azure-sql-binding-func-dotnet-todo/todo-backend-dotnet-azure-sql-bindings-azure-functions/)\\n2. [Use SQL bindings in Azure Stream Analytics](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/stream-analytics/sql-database-upsert#option-1-update-by-key-with-the-azure-function-sql-binding)\\n# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-python)\\nThis set of articles explains how to work with [Azure SQL](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/index) bindings in Azure Functions. Azure Functions supports input bindings, output bindings, and a function trigger for the Azure SQL and SQL Server products.\\n| Action | Type| \\n|  --- | ---  |\\n| Trigger a function when a change is detected on a SQL table | [SQL trigger](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-trigger) |\\n| Read data from a database | [Input binding](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-input) |\\n| Save data to a database | [Output binding](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-output) |\\n\",\"contentUrl\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql#samples\"},{\"title\":\"Azure Functions libraries for .NET\",\"content\":\"# Azure Functions libraries for .NET\\n## Overview\\n[Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview) is a solution for easily running small pieces of code, or *functions*, in Azure. Azure Functions supports a [serverless](https://azure.microsoft.com/solutions/serverless/) execution model.\\n## Durable Functions extension\\n*Durable Functions* is an extension of [Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview) that lets you write stateful functions in a serverless compute environment. The extension lets you define stateful workflows by writing [*orchestrator functions*](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/durable/durable-functions-orchestrations) and stateful entities by writing [*entity functions*](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/durable/durable-functions-entities) using the Azure Functions programming model. Behind the scenes, the extension manages state, checkpoints, and restarts for you, allowing you to focus on your business logic.\\nInstall the extension [NuGet package](https://www.nuget.org/packages/Microsoft.Azure.WebJobs.Extensions.DurableTask) directly from the Visual Studio [Package Manager console](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/nuget/tools/package-manager-console) or with the [.NET Core CLI](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/tools/dotnet-add-package).\\nThe Azure API reference documentation supports [version 2.x of the Durable Functions extension](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/overview/azure/functions/runtime).\\n### Visual Studio package manager\\n\\u0060\\u0060\\u0060powershell\\nInstall-Package Microsoft.Azure.WebJobs.Extensions.DurableTask\\n\\u0060\\u0060\\u0060\\n### .NET Core CLI\\n\\u0060\\u0060\\u0060dotnetcli\\ndotnet add package Microsoft.Azure.WebJobs.Extensions.DurableTask\\n\\u0060\\u0060\\u0060\\n### Getting started\\n## Samples\\nView the [complete list](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/browse/?products=azure-functions\\u0026languages=csharp) of Azure Functions samples.\",\"contentUrl\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/overview/azure/functions?view=azure-dotnet#overview\"},{\"title\":\"Overview of Azure functions for Azure Redis (programming-language-java)\",\"content\":\"# Overview of Azure functions for Azure Redis (programming-language-java)\\nThis article describes how to use either Azure Managed Redis or Azure Cache for Redis with Azure Functions to create optimized serverless and event-driven architectures.\\nAzure Functions provides an event-driven programming model where triggers and bindings are key features. With Azure Functions, you can easily build event-driven serverless applications. Azure Redis services (Azure Managed Redis and Azure Cache for Redis) provide a set of building blocks and best practices for building distributed applications, including microservices, state management, pub/sub messaging, and more.\\nAzure Redis can be used as a trigger for Azure Functions, allowing you to initiate a serverless workflow. This functionality can be highly useful in data architectures like a write-behind cache, or any event-based architectures.\\nYou can integrate Azure Redis and Azure Functions to build functions that react to events from Azure Redis or external systems.\\n| Action | Direction| \\n|  --- | ---  |\\n| [Trigger on Redis pub sub messages](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-cache-trigger-redispubsub) | Trigger |\\n| [Trigger on Redis lists](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-cache-trigger-redislist) | Trigger |\\n| [Trigger on Redis streams](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-cache-trigger-redisstream) | Trigger |\\n| [Read a cached value](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-cache-input) | Input |\\n| [Write a values to cache](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-cache-output) | Output |\\n\\n## Scope of availability for functions triggers and bindings\\n| Tier | Azure Cache for Redis (Basic, Standard, Premium, Enterprise, Enterprise Flash) | Azure Managed Redis (Memory Optimized, Basic, Compute Optimized, Flash Optimized)| \\n|  --- | --- | ---  |\\n| Pub/Sub | Yes | Yes |\\n| Lists | Yes | Yes |\\n| Streams | Yes | Yes |\\n| Bindings | Yes | Yes |\\n\\nImportant\\nRedis triggers are currently only supported for functions running in either a [Elastic Premium plan](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-premium-plan) or a dedicated [App Service plan](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/dedicated-plan).\",\"contentUrl\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-cache\"},{\"title\":\"Get started with Azure Functions (programming-language-csharp)\",\"content\":\"# Get started with Azure Functions (programming-language-csharp)\\nAzure Functions lets you implement your system\\u0027s logic as event-driven, readily available blocks of code. These code blocks are called functions. This article helps you find the most useful Azure Functions content quickly. For more general information about Azure Functions, see the [Introduction to Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview).\\nChoose your preferred development language at the top of the article.\\n## Create your first function\\nComplete a quickstart article to create and deploy your first functions in less than five minutes.\\nYou can create your first function by using one of the following tools:\\n1. [Azure Developer CLI (azd)](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/create-first-function-azure-developer-cli)\\n2. [Command line](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-azure-cli)\\n3. [Visual Studio Code](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-vs-code)\\n4. [Visual Studio](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-create-your-first-function-visual-studio)\\n## Review end-to-end samples\\nThese sites let you browse existing functions reference projects and samples in your desired language:\\n1. [Awesome azd template library](https://azure.github.io/awesome-azd/?tags=functions\\u0026tags=dotnetCsharp)\\n2. [Azure Functions samples in GitHub](https://github.com/search?q=topic%3Aazure-functions\\u002Borg%3AAzure-Samples\\u002Bfork%3Atrue\\u002Blanguage%3AC%23\\u0026type=repositories\\u0026l=C%23)\\n3. [Azure samples browser](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/browse/?expanded=azure\\u0026languages=csharp\\u0026products=azure-functions)\\n## Scenarios\\nHere are some common scenarios to help you get started:\\n1. [Process file uploads](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#process-file-uploads)\\n2. [Real-time stream and event processing](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#real-time-stream-and-event-processing)\\n3. [Build AI-enabled apps](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-ai-enabled-apps)\\n4. [Run scheduled tasks](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#run-scheduled-tasks)\\n5. [Build a scalable web API](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-a-scalable-web-api)\\n6. [Build a serverless workflow](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-a-serverless-workflow)\\n7. [Respond to database changes](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#respond-to-database-changes)\\n8. [Create reliable message systems](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#create-reliable-message-systems)\",\"contentUrl\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-get-started\"},{\"title\":\"Get started with Azure Functions (programming-language-javascript)\",\"content\":\"# Get started with Azure Functions (programming-language-javascript)\\nAzure Functions lets you implement your system\\u0027s logic as event-driven, readily available blocks of code. These code blocks are called functions. This article helps you find the most useful Azure Functions content quickly. For more general information about Azure Functions, see the [Introduction to Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview).\\nChoose your preferred development language at the top of the article.\\n## Create your first function\\nComplete a quickstart article to create and deploy your first functions in less than five minutes.\\nYou can create your first function by using one of the following tools:\\n1. [Azure Developer CLI (azd)](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/create-first-function-azure-developer-cli)\\n2. [Command line](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-azure-cli)\\n3. [Visual Studio Code](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-vs-code)\\n## Review end-to-end samples\\nThese sites let you browse existing functions reference projects and samples in your desired language:\\n1. [Awesome azd template library](https://azure.github.io/awesome-azd/?tags=functions\\u0026tags=javascript)\\n2. [Azure Functions samples in GitHub](https://github.com/search?q=topic%3Aazure-functions\\u002Borg%3AAzure-Samples\\u002Bfork%3Atrue\\u002Blanguage%3AJavaScript\\u0026type=repositories)\\n3. [Azure samples browser](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/browse/?expanded=azure\\u0026languages=javascript\\u0026products=azure-functions)\\n## Scenarios\\nHere are some common scenarios to help you get started:\\n1. [Process file uploads](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#process-file-uploads)\\n2. [Real-time stream and event processing](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#real-time-stream-and-event-processing)\\n3. [Build AI-enabled apps](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-ai-enabled-apps)\\n4. [Run scheduled tasks](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#run-scheduled-tasks)\\n5. [Build a scalable web API](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-a-scalable-web-api)\\n6. [Build a serverless workflow](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-a-serverless-workflow)\\n7. [Respond to database changes](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#respond-to-database-changes)\\n8. [Create reliable message systems](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#create-reliable-message-systems)\",\"contentUrl\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-get-started\"},{\"title\":\"Azure Functions diagnostics overview\",\"content\":\"# Azure Functions diagnostics overview\\nWhen you\\u2019re running a function app, you want to be prepared for any issues that may arise, from 4xx errors to trigger failures. Azure Functions diagnostics is an intelligent and interactive experience to help you troubleshoot your function app with no configuration or extra cost. When you do run into issues with your function app, Azure Functions diagnostics points out what\\u2019s wrong. It guides you to the right information to more easily and quickly troubleshoot and resolve the issue. This article shows you the basics of how to use Azure Functions diagnostics to more quickly diagnose and solve common function app issues.\\n## Start Azure Functions diagnostics\\nTo start Azure Functions diagnostics:\\n1. Navigate to your function app in the [Azure portal](https://portal.azure.com).\\n2. Select **Diagnose and solve problems** to open Azure Functions diagnostics.\\n3. Choose a category that best describes the issue of your function app by using the keywords in the homepage tile. You can also type a keyword that best describes your issue in the search bar. For example, you could type \\u0060execution\\u0060 to see a list of diagnostic reports related to your function app execution and open them directly from the homepage.\\n ![Search for Azure Functions diagnostics.](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/media/functions-diagnostics/functions-app-search-azure-functions-diagnostics.png)\\n## Use the Interactive interface\\nOnce you select a homepage category that best aligns with your function app\\u0027s problem, Azure Functions diagnostics\\u0027 interactive interface, named Genie, can guide you through diagnosing and solving problem of your app. You can use the tile shortcuts provided by Genie to view the full diagnostic report of the problem category that you\\u0027re interested in. The tile shortcuts provide you a direct way of accessing your diagnostic metrics.\\n![Genie is Azure Functions diagnostics\\u0027 interface.](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/media/functions-diagnostics/genie.png)\\nAfter selecting a tile, you can see a list of topics related to the issue described in the tile. These topics provide snippets of notable information from the full report. Select any of these topics to investigate the issues further. Also, you can select **View Full Report** to explore all the topics on a single page.\\n![Preview of diagnostic report](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/media/functions-diagnostics/preview-of-diagnostic-report.png)\",\"contentUrl\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-diagnostics\"},{\"title\":\"Get started with Azure Functions (programming-language-go)\",\"content\":\"# Get started with Azure Functions (programming-language-go)\\nAzure Functions lets you implement your system\\u0027s logic as event-driven, readily available blocks of code. These code blocks are called functions. This article helps you find the most useful Azure Functions content quickly. For more general information about Azure Functions, see the [Introduction to Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview).\\nChoose your preferred development language at the top of the article.\\n## Create your first function\\nComplete a quickstart article to create and deploy your first functions in less than five minutes.\\nYou can create your first function by using one of the following tools:\\n1. [Azure Developer CLI (azd)](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/create-first-function-azure-developer-cli)\\n2. [Command line](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-azure-cli)\\n3. [Visual Studio Code](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-vs-code)\\nGet started with Go on Azure Functions:\\n1. [Command line quickstart](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/how-to-create-function-azure-cli?pivots=programming-language-go)\\n## Review end-to-end samples\\n1. [Azure Functions Go worker samples](https://github.com/Azure/azure-functions-golang-worker/tree/main/samples)\\n## Scenarios\\nHere are some common scenarios to help you get started:\\n1. [Process file uploads](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#process-file-uploads)\\n2. [Real-time stream and event processing](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#real-time-stream-and-event-processing)\\n3. [Build AI-enabled apps](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-ai-enabled-apps)\\n4. [Run scheduled tasks](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#run-scheduled-tasks)\\n5. [Build a scalable web API](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-a-scalable-web-api)\\n6. [Build a serverless workflow](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#build-a-serverless-workflow)\\n7. [Respond to database changes](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#respond-to-database-changes)\\n8. [Create reliable message systems](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-scenarios#create-reliable-message-systems)\\n## Explore an interactive tutorial\\nComplete one of the following interactive training modules to learn more about Functions:\\n1. [Well-Architected Framework - Performance efficiency](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/azure-well-architected-performance-efficiency/)\\n2. [Azure Functions triggers and bindings](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-triggers-bindings)\\nTo learn even more, see the [full listing of interactive tutorials](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/browse/?expanded=azure\\u0026products=azure-functions).\",\"contentUrl\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-get-started\"}]}","call_tool_result_json":"{\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"{\\\"results\\\":[{\\\"title\\\":\\\"What is Azure Functions?\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"# What is Azure Functions?\\\\nAzure Functions is a serverless solution that allows you to build robust apps while using less code, and with less infrastructure and lower costs. Instead of worrying about deploying and maintaining servers, you can use the cloud infrastructure to provide all the up-to-date resources needed to keep your applications running.\\\\nYou focus on the code that matters most to you, in the most productive language for you, and Azure Functions handles the rest. For a list of supported languages, see [Supported languages in Azure Functions](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/supported-languages).\\\",\\\"contentUrl\\\":\\\"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-csharp)\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-csharp)\\\\n## Samples\\\\nIn addition to the samples for C#, Java, JavaScript, PowerShell, and Python available in the [Azure SQL bindings GitHub repository](https://github.com/Azure/azure-functions-sql-extension/tree/main/samples), more are available in Azure Samples:\\\\n1. [ToDo API sample with Azure SQL bindings (C#)](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/samples/azure-samples/azure-sql-binding-func-dotnet-todo/todo-backend-dotnet-azure-sql-bindings-azure-functions/)\\\\n2. [Use SQL bindings in Azure Stream Analytics](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/stream-analytics/sql-database-upsert#option-1-update-by-key-with-the-azure-function-sql-binding)\\\\n# Azure SQL bindings for Azure Functions overview (programming-language-javascript)\\\\nThis set of articles explains how to work with [Azure SQL](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/index) bindings in Azure Functions. Azure Functions supports input bindings, output bindings, and a function trigger for the Azure SQL and SQL Server products.\\\\n| Action